"Изв. Акад. Наук СССР (https://www.mathnet.ru/links/8b74f3ed1a7d3d4a2269c7b144f7f633/im1268_eng.pdf)\nciteturn14search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 21.4 years ago; Finally, the three arguments conjecture is refuted for commutative Moufang loops. In fact, an associator is constructed that is not the identity but has three of its arguments the same.\nИзв. Акад. Наук СССР  \nСер. Матем. Том 51(1987), № 1\n\nMath. USSR Izvestiya  \nVol. 30(1988), No. 1\n\nUDC 519.48\n\nINFINITE IRREDUNDANT SYSTEMS OF IDENTITIES  \nIN COMMUTATIVE MOUFANG LOOPS  \nAND DISTRIBUTIVE STEINER QUASIGROUPS\n\nN. I. SANDU\n\n**Abstract.** An infinite irredundant system of identities in the variety of commutative Moufang loops (and in the variety of distributive Steiner quasigroups) is constructed with the property that no infinite subset is equivalent to a finite subsystem. It follows that the set of subvarieties of the variety of commutative Moufang loops (and of the variety of distributive Steiner quasigroups) has the cardinal of the continuum; it also follows that there is a commutative Moufang loop (a distributive Steiner quasigroup) given by an enumerable set of identical relations, in which the word problem is unsolvable. Finally, the three arguments conjecture is refuted for commutative Moufang loops. In fact, an associator is constructed that is not the identity but has three of its arguments the same.  \nBibliography: 10 titles.\n\n**Introduction**\n\nOne of the important questions in the study of varieties of algebraic systems is that of determining the cardinal numbers of bases of identities. Evans proved in [1] that the set of identities in a centrally nilpotent commutative Moufang loop (CML for short) is finitely based; that is, they are all consequences of finitely many of them. The Bruck-Slaby theorem [2] gives as a corollary that a finitely generated CML has a finite basis for its identities. This result was extended to groupoids in [3]. It was shown that every finitely generated distributive (or trimedial) quasigroup, and also every finitely generated commutative distributive groupoid, has a finite basis for its identities.\n\nA system of quasigroup identities is said to be irredundant if no identity in the system follows from the others. The main aim of this paper is to construct infinite irredundant systems of identities, as in the title. From this we get the existence of continuum many distinct varieties of CML and of distributive Steiner quasigroups (DSQ for short). We give a CML (and a DSQ) defined by an enumerable set of identities in which the word problem is unsolvable. We note that the solvability of the word problem for a finitely generated CML was proved in [1].\n\nIn [4], Smith propounded the conjecture that the associators of a CML with arguments occurring three times are the identity. He called this the three argument conjecture. It was claimed in [4] that the conjecture can be confirmed using the methods of [6]. The three arguments conjecture for Moufang loops is disproved here *en route* to a proof of the main result of the article.\n\n1980 Mathematics Subject Classification (1985 Revision). Primary 20N05.\n\n©1988 American Mathematical Society  \n0025-5726/88 $1.00 + $.25 per page\n\n163--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInfinite Irredundant Systems of Identities in Commutative Moufang Loops and Distributive Steiner Quasigroups - Science Explorer Abstract (https://scixplorer.org/abs/1988IzMat..30..163S/abstract)\nciteturn14search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 38.8 years ago; Crawled: 5 days ago; Finally, the three arguments conjecture is refuted for commutative Moufang loops. In fact, an associator is constructed that is not the identity but has three of its arguments the same.\n\n## Infinite Irredundant Systems of Identities in Commutative Moufang Loops and Distributive Steiner Quasigroups\n\n### Authors\n\nSandu, N. I.\n\nRefereed Article\n\n### Abstract\n\nAn infinite irr